Implementing a multi-tree list in an Angular application involves several steps. A multi-tree list is essentially a tree structure where each node can have multiple child nodes, and these children can also be trees themselves, creating a hierarchical structure.

Here's a basic example of how you might implement this using the `@angular/cdk` library for the TreeList component and some custom logic for handling the tree data.

### Step 1: Set Up Your Project

First, make sure you have the necessary dependencies installed:

```bash
npm install @angular/cli @angular/cdk @angular/material rxjs
```

Then create your Angular project if it doesn't already exist:

```bash
ng new multiTreeListApp
cd multiTreeListApp
```

### Step 2: Create the Tree Data Model

Create a file named `data.ts` in the `src/app` directory:

```typescript
import { TreeItem } from '@angular/cdk/tree';

export class TreeNode {
    id: number;
    label: string;
    children?: Array<TreeNode>;
}

const treeData = [
    {
        id: 0,
        label: 'Root',
        children: [
            {
                id: 1,
                label: 'Child 1'
            },
            {
                id: 2,
                label: 'Child 2',
                children: [
                    {
                        id: 3,
                        label: 'Grandchild 1'
                    }
                ]
            }
        ]
    }
];

export function getTreeNodes(treeData: any[]): TreeItem[] {
    return treeData.map((node) => ({
        key: `${node.id}`,
        label: node.label,
        expanded: false,
        hasChildren: !!node.children.length,
        children: node.children && getTreeNodes(node.children),
    }));
}
```

### Step 3: Define the Tree List Component

Create a component that will use the TreeList. For simplicity, we'll name it `MultiTreeListComponent`.

In `src/app/multitree-list.component.ts`, define the component:

```typescript
import { Component } from '@angular/core';
import * as Rx from 'rxjs/Rx';
import { Observable } from 'rxjs/Observable';
import { TreeItem } from '@angular/cdk/tree';
import { getTreeNodes } from './data';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-multitree-list',
  template: `
    <mat-tree [dataSource]="treeDataSource" [treeTemplate]="treeTemplate">
      <ng-template matTreeNodeContent let-node>
        {{ node.label }}
      </ng-template>
    </mat-tree>
  `,
  styles: []
})
export class MultiTreeListComponent {
  treeDataSource: Observable<MatTreeNode[]> | undefined;

  constructor() {}

  ngOnInit(): void {
    // Subscribe to changes in the tree data
    const observable$ = Rx.Observable.fromEvent(this.treeDataSource, 'expand').map(() => {
      this.updateTree();
    });
    observable$.subscribe();
  }

  updateTree() {
    this.treeDataSource = getTreeNodes(treeData);
  }
}
```

### Step 4: Update the Main Application Template

Finally, update the main app template (`src/app/app.component.html`) to include the `MultiTreeListComponent`:

```html
<div style="margin-top: 50px;">
  <app-multitree-list></app-multitree-list>
</div>
```

### Step 5: Run Your Application

Start the development server:

```bash
ng serve
```

Open your browser to `http://localhost:4200/` and you should see a simple multi-tree list displaying the hierarchy provided in your `data.ts` file.

